```java
class ConfigLoader {
    private static final class Holder {
        static final ConfigLoader INSTANCE = new ConfigLoader();
    }

    static ConfigLoader getInstance() {
        return Holder.INSTANCE;
    }

    private ConfigLoader() { /* expensive setup */ }
}
```

This is the Java **static holder idiom**: the JVM guarantees a class is initialized exactly once, lazily, the first time it's referenced -- so `Holder.INSTANCE` is created on the first call to `getInstance()`, safely published, with no explicit lock at all.

The older, more error-prone approach -- **double-checked locking** -- checks if the instance is null, locks, checks again, then creates it, to avoid paying lock overhead on every call after the first. It's easy to get subtly wrong (the object can be seen partially constructed) without an explicit memory-visibility guarantee (Java's `volatile` on the field, specifically); the static holder idiom sidesteps that hazard entirely by relying on class-loading guarantees instead of hand-rolled locking.
